\par\par \b \uc2  Nigeria to open up economy to attract investment. \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Jukwey \par \uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LAGOS, Jan 14 (Reuter) - Military ruler General Sani Abacha, in his much-delayed 1995 budget, on Saturday threw open Nigeria's economy in a bid to attract local and foreign investment. \par \par \uc2  "The 1995 budget is a budget of incentives; and a budget of liberalisation," Abacha said in a radio and television broadcast. \par \par \uc2  In the broadcast, copies of which were distributed in advance, Abacha announced that the autonomous \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, hitherto banned, would now be permitted. \par \par \uc2  He repealed the 1962 Exchange Control Act "to allow the inflow of funds" and the 1989 Enterprises Promotion Decree which barred foreigners from holding majority stakes in many businesses. \par \par \uc2  "Government shall examine all laws, regulations, rules, etc that hinder the free flow of investments and funds into Nigeria," Abacha said, adding "Such rules will be repealed and the regulations relaxed." \par \par \uc2  He said government was also willing to enter into bilateral agreements with foreign governments to ensure the investments of their nationals in Nigeria were not tampered with. \par \par \uc2  Abacha's broadcast comes two weeks behind the traditional budget day of January 1 and has been eagerly awaited by local and foreign businesses. \par \par \uc2  It marks a return to free market reforms which Nigeria abandoned in Abacha's first budget in January 1994 after he seized power in November, 1993. \par \par \uc2  In that budget, he fixed the naira exchange rate at 22 to the dollar, a fraction of its illegal but thriving black-market level, and capped interest rates at 21 percent. \par \par \uc2  In the new budget he said "the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will henceforth be subject to guided delegulation, as shall interest rate regime." \par \par \uc2  This should please local business groups which have, along with Nigeria's foreign creditors and international finance agencies like the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank, been calling for deregulation. \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rate policy in 1995 is designed to maintain the current official rate and at the same time permit the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the autonomous market," Abacha said. \par \par \uc2  "To this end, the autonomous market will now be permitted in Nigeria and the inter-bank autonomous market shall be allowed to operate freely," he added. \par \par \uc2  This means Nigerians and foreigners are no longer compelled to bring \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and can open domiciliary accounts to which, Abacha said, they would have unfettered access. \par \par \uc2  The government has retained the 22 naira to the dollar official exchange rate but suspended hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at the official market. \par \par \uc2  It was not immediately clear what the relationship between the official and the autonomous markets would be. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister is due to explain how the the new \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will operate when he gives the budget breakdown on Monday. \par \par \uc2  On interest rates, Abacha said there would be modifications to allow flexibility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of last year's policy. \par \par \uc2  "Under the new arrangements, banks and other financial institutions are required to maintain a maximum spread of 7.5 percent between their deposit and lending rates subject to a maximum lending rate of 21 percent," Abacha said. \par \par \uc2  He said revenue estimates for 1995 were based on an average price of $15 a barrel for crude oil, Nigeria's main export and that at an exchange rate of 22 naira to the dollar Nigeria should earn more 200 billion naira ($9 billion) from the commodity. \par \par \uc2  His government would also boost the non-oil sector, essentially solid minerals and agriculture from which it expected to earn 150 billion naira ($6.8 billion). \par \par \uc2  External debt servicing will take up 44 billion naira ($2 billion) while Nigeria will try to conclude negotiations with the IMF and the World Bank to ratify its medium-term economic programme so that it can secure debt relief. \par \par \uc2  Abacha promised to scrap so-called dedicated accounts where oil sales are lodged for specific projects but which a probe panel found had been largely looted. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr1f08a1p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
