\par\par \b \uc2  Nigeria military ruler opens economy in 95 budget. \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Jukwey \par \uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LAGOS, Jan 14 (Reuter) - Nigeria's military ruler announced his government's delayed 1995 budget on Saturday after a year of political and economic turmoil, promising moves back to market reforms and less restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. \par \par \uc2  General Sani Abacha also pledged to continue repayments on Nigeria's mounting foreign debts. \par \par \uc2  "The 1995 budget is a budget of incentives, and a budget of liberalisation," Abacha said in a radio and television broadcast. \par \par \uc2  He repealed the 1962 Exchange Control Act "to allow the inflow of funds" and a 1989 decree which barred foreigners from holding majority stakes in many businesses. \par \par \uc2  "Government shall examine all laws, regulations, rules, etc that hinder the free flow of investments and funds into Nigeria," Abacha said. \par \par \uc2  "Such rules will be repealed and the regulations relaxed." \par \par \uc2  In his speech, advance copies of which were made available, Abacha said the autonomous \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market -- among banks and exchange bureaux -- would no longer be banned. \par \par \uc2  But he said the government would stick to its officially set exchange rate of 22 naira to the dollar, fixed one year ago. \par \par \uc2  Abacha seized power in November, 1993, and later abandoned market reforms backed by the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rate policy in 1995 is designed to maintain the current official rate and at the same time permit the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the autonomous market," Abacha said. \par \par \uc2  "To this end, the autonomous market will now be permitted in Nigeria and the inter-bank autonomous market shall be allowed to operate freely," he added. \par \par \uc2  It was not clear how the government would deal with the wide disparity between the official and current black market rates. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will henceforth be subject to guided deregulation, as shall interest rate regime," he said. \par \par \uc2  Nigerians and foreigners are no longer compelled to bring in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and can open local accounts to which they would have unrestricted access, the General said. \par \par \uc2  The military government appears to have subdued its political opponents in 1994 but Nigerian newspaper commentators agree that last year, marked by pro-democracy strikes and protests, was one of immense political and economic anguish for most of the country's 90 million people. \par \par \uc2  Abacha's broadcast comes two weeks behind the traditional budget day of January 1 and has been eagerly awaited. The finance minister is due to explain how the the new \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will operate when he gives the budget breakdown on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Local business groups, foreign creditors and international finance agencies like the IMF and World Bank have called for deregulation but powerful trade unions say market reforms would worsen the plight of ordinary Nigerians. \par \par \uc2  Abacha said the government would continue to devote about 30 percent of anticipated official hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings to debt servicing and that 44 billion naira ($2 billion at the official exchange rate) had been earmarked for debt servicing in 1995. \par \par \uc2  Nigeria's total foreign debt stood at $29.43 billion at the end of October 1994. \par \par \uc2  "Due to the dwindling resources of government and the heavy debt service burden, the build up of payments arrears on external loans deteriorated during the fiscal year," he said, without giving figures. \par \par \uc2  Abacha said the government had suspended the sale of shares in public companies because of "some indentified inadequacies and the huge losses incurred". \par \par \uc2  Instead he announced plans to lease out state enterprises to local and foreign businesses. \par \par \uc2  The federal government's net revenue in 1995 was expected to total 160.86 billion naira, he said. \par \par \uc2  Revenue estimates for 1995 were based on an average price of $15 a barrel for crude oil, Nigeria's main export, and that at the current exchange rate Nigeria should earn more than 200 billion naira ($9 billion) from oil, Abacha said. \par \par \uc2  It was not clear from his speech whether the government was aiming for a balanced budget following a large deficit in 1994. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr1e089jz\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (nigeria near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\br